<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Lampang</h1><p class="st-meta">1732 CE – 1899 CE (approx.) · capital Lampang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Thai principality whose Chet Ton dynasty led the expulsion of the Burmese from Lan Na and later ruled Chiang Mai.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1732 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nan-thip-chang">Nan Thip Chang</a>, a hunter turned local leader, drove out a Burmese-backed governor from Lampang and was recognised as ruler. His descendants, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chet-ton-dynasty">Chet Ton</a> or Chao Chet Ton dynasty, governed Lampang under Burmese overlordship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lampang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing principality on the Wang river with the revered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-that-lampang-luang">Wat Phra That Lampang Luang</a>. In the 1770s its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a> and helped drive the Burmese out of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a>. Thereafter Lampang was a Siamese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and Kawila's family also governed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-chiang-mai">Chiang Mai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lampang kept its own princes during the 19th century, while British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> firms logged teak in its forests. The provincial reforms of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom</a> brought it under direct administration from 1899, and Lampang is now a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-lampang.jpg" alt="Principality of Lampang" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: YashiWong · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:10340_Lampang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1732 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lampang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nan Thip Chang, Kawila</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Northern Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nakhon Lampang, Lampang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1816&amp;focus=principality-of-lampang" aria-label="Principality of Lampang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7377 1499 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Lampang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7697 1755L7754 1922"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7754 1922h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7697 1755h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7697 1755h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1816&amp;focus=principality-of-lampang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
